    <header>
      <h1>HTTPS and connection security</h1>
      <p>What encryption protects, how certificates support identity, and why a secure connection is only part of the story.</p>
    </header>
    
      
        <section id="protection"><h2>HTTP with a protected connection</h2><p>HTTPS uses HTTP over a secured connection. The application still exchanges methods, headers, status codes, and content. TLS provides the security properties that ordinary unencrypted HTTP lacks. <a href="references.html#rfc9110">[5]</a></p>
          <dl><dt>Confidentiality</dt><dd>Encryption makes application data unreadable to someone merely observing the network path.</dd><dt>Integrity</dt><dd>Cryptographic protection helps detect unauthorized changes to data in transit.</dd><dt>Authentication</dt><dd>Certificate validation helps a browser establish that the server is authorized for the requested hostname.</dd></dl><p class="source-note">TLS security goals: <a href="references.html#rfc8446">RFC 8446 [6]</a>.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="tls"><h2>TLS and the certificate chain</h2><p>In a simplified TLS handshake, the client and server negotiate security parameters and establish shared keys. The server normally supplies a certificate, and the browser checks the hostname and a chain of trust to a trusted authority. TLS 1.3 protects application data using negotiated symmetric keys. <a href="references.html#rfc8446">[6]</a></p>
          <figure><img src="assets/https-layers.svg" width="960" height="400" loading="lazy" alt="HTTP defines the meaning of requests and responses. TLS protects the connection. TCP carries HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, while HTTP/3 uses QUIC with TLS 1.3 integrated."><figcaption>Figure 2. A simplified view of HTTP and security. QUIC integrates TLS 1.3 rather than placing a separate TLS record layer above TCP. Based on RFC 8446 and RFC 9114.</figcaption></figure>
          <p>A valid certificate helps establish the server’s identity for a domain. It does not prove that the website’s owner is honest or that every page is safe. Users should still check the exact domain and treat unexpected requests for credentials cautiously.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="limits"><h2>What HTTPS does not solve</h2><ul><li>A phishing website can use HTTPS and a valid certificate for its own domain.</li><li>Encryption in transit does not prevent vulnerable application code or compromised accounts.</li><li>Protection on the connection does not determine how the server stores or shares information afterward.</li></ul><aside class="note"><h3>Connection security and website trust</h3><p>HTTPS protects communication with the identified server. Safe use also depends on the application, the user’s device, and the organization handling the data.</p></aside></section>
        <section id="practice"><h2>Example: submitting a form</h2><p>Imagine entering information into an inquiry form. With HTTPS, the connection protects the submission in transit. The server must still validate the input, restrict access, and store it appropriately. A POST request alone does not encrypt a submission; the protected connection matters.</p><p>To explore this distinction, inspect the connection information for a familiar HTTPS site. Find the hostname and certificate details. Browser interfaces differ, so the location of these controls can vary.</p></section>
        <p>Keep learning <a href="concepts.html">Explore the seven key concepts ↗</a></p>
      
    
